\title{The Extremal Regime of Adjacent-Vertex-Distinguishing Total Colourings of
Central Graphs of Regular Graphs}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-14}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
For a finite simple graph $G$, the \emph{central graph} $C(G)$ is obtained by
subdividing every edge of $G$ once and joining every pair of non-adjacent vertices
of $G$. We study the \emph{adjacent-vertex-distinguishing (AVD) total chromatic
number} $\avd(C(G))$, the least number of colours needed to colour both the
vertices and edges of $C(G)$ so that the colouring is a proper total colouring and
any two adjacent vertices receive distinct colour sets (a vertex's own colour
together with the colours of its incident edges). For a $d$-regular graph $G$ that
is not complete we establish two lower bounds: a degree-governed bound
$\avd(C(G)) \ge d + 3$ and a vertex-governed bound $\avd(C(G)) \ge |V(G)| + 1$.
Since a non-complete $d$-regular graph satisfies $|V(G)| \ge d + 2$, the
vertex-governed bound always dominates, and the two coincide exactly in the
\emph{extremal regime} $|V(G)| = d + 2$. Our main structural result characterises
this regime: a non-complete $d$-regular graph is extremal if and only if its
complement is $1$-regular, i.e. $G$ is a cocktail-party graph $K_{d+2}$ minus a
perfect matching. On this family the two lower bounds collapse to the single sharp
value $d + 3 = |V(G)| + 1$. We illustrate the theory on cycles: $C_4$ is the
smallest extremal instance (its central graph requires at least $5$ colours),
whereas $C_5$ lies strictly outside the extremal family, exhibiting the strict
domination $d + 3 < |V(G)| + 1$.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Colouring problems that treat vertices and edges on an equal footing --- \emph{total
colourings} --- sit at the confluence of classical chromatic theory and
scheduling. A refinement of increasing interest adds a \emph{distinguishability}
requirement: adjacent vertices must be told apart by the entire set of colours in
their immediate neighbourhood. This is the \emph{adjacent-vertex-distinguishing
total colouring}, and its optimal size is the AVD-total chromatic number $\avd$.

The \emph{central graph} construction transforms a base graph $G$ by combining
barycentric subdivision of edges with complementation on the original vertex set.
It produces graphs with a highly structured degree profile --- the original
vertices become high-degree hubs, the edge-vertices low-degree connectors ---
making central graphs an excellent testbed for chromatic invariants.

A guiding conjecture asserts that for a $d$-regular non-complete graph $G$,
$\avd(C(G)) = d + 3$. In this paper we make the \emph{lower} half of this equality
precise and delineate exactly where the equality can hold. We isolate two competing
lower bounds, show that the vertex-governed bound dominates, and characterise the
extremal boundary where the two agree. The conjectured value $d + 3$ is correct
only on a thin, explicitly described family, and is strictly too small everywhere
else.

\section{Total colourings, the total graph, and the AVD condition}

Throughout, all graphs are finite and simple. For a graph $H$ we write $V(H)$ for
its vertex set and $E(H)$ for its edge set.

\begin{definition}[Total graph]
The \emph{total graph} $T(H)$ has vertex set $V(H) \sqcup E(H)$, with adjacency:
$a \sim b$ for $a, b \in V(H)$ iff $a, b$ are adjacent in $H$;
$a \sim e$ for $a \in V(H)$, $e \in E(H)$ iff $a \in e$;
$e \sim f$ for $e, f \in E(H)$ iff $e \ne f$ and $e, f$ share an endpoint.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Total colouring]
A \emph{total colouring} of $H$ with palette $\kappa$ is a proper vertex colouring
of $T(H)$: adjacent vertices of $H$ differ, incident edges differ, and a vertex
differs from each incident edge.
\end{definition}

Fix $w \in V(H)$. The \emph{star} at $w$ consists of $w$ together with all edges of
$H$ incident to $w$, viewed as vertices of $T(H)$; it has $\deg_H(w) + 1$ elements.

\begin{lemma}[Star clique]\label{lem:star}
The star at $w$ is a clique in $T(H)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The vertex $w$ is adjacent to each incident edge by incidence, and any two distinct
edges incident to $w$ share the endpoint $w$, hence are adjacent in $T(H)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Injectivity on the star]\label{cor:inj}
In any total colouring, the colours of the star members at $w$ are pairwise
distinct; hence at least $\deg_H(w) + 1$ colours occur on the star at $w$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Colour set and AVD]
For a total colouring $C$, the \emph{colour set} of $w$ is
$\Cs(w) = \{C(w)\} \cup \{C(e) : e \in E(H),\ w \in e\}$. The colouring is
\emph{adjacent-vertex-distinguishing (AVD)} if $\Cs(a) \ne \Cs(b)$ whenever $a, b$
are adjacent in $H$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Full palette at tight vertices]\label{lem:full}
If the palette has exactly $\deg_H(w) + 1$ colours, then $\Cs(w)$ equals the entire
palette in any total colouring.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:inj} the $\deg_H(w) + 1$ star members receive pairwise
distinct colours; with only $\deg_H(w) + 1$ colours, all must occur.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Equal-degree obstruction]\label{prop:obstruction}
Let $u, v$ be adjacent in $H$ with $\deg_H(u) = \deg_H(v) = \Delta$. Then no
AVD-total colouring of $H$ uses only $\Delta + 1$ colours.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
With $\Delta + 1$ colours, Lemma~\ref{lem:full} forces
$\Cs(u) = \Cs(v) = $ the whole palette, so the signatures coincide, violating the
AVD condition on $uv$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Palette monotonicity]\label{lem:mono}
If $H$ has an AVD-total colouring with $n$ colours and $n \le m$, then it has one
with $m$ colours.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Embed $\{1,\dots,n\}$ into $\{1,\dots,m\}$ via an injection $\iota$ and recolour by
$\iota \circ C$. Properness is preserved by injectivity, and each signature is
transported by $\iota$; since $\iota$ is injective on sets, distinct signatures
stay distinct.
\end{proof}

Consequently $\avd(H)$ is a well-defined threshold: a colouring with $n$ colours
exists iff $n \ge \avd(H)$.

\section{The central graph}

\begin{definition}[Central graph]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph with vertex set $V$. The \emph{central graph}
$C(G)$ has vertex set $V \sqcup E(G)$, with adjacency: $u \sim w$ for $u, w \in V$
iff $u \ne w$ and $u, w$ non-adjacent in $G$; $u \sim e$ for $u \in V$,
$e \in E(G)$ iff $u \in e$; and no two edge-vertices are adjacent.
\end{definition}

We call vertices in $V$ \emph{original} and those in $E(G)$ \emph{edge-vertices}.
The induced subgraph on $V$ is the complement $G^{c}$.

\begin{proposition}[Degree of original vertices]\label{prop:deg}
Every original vertex of $C(G)$ has degree $|V| - 1$; equivalently
$\deg_{C(G)}(v) + 1 = |V(G)|$ for all $v \in V$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The neighbours of $v$ split into the original vertices non-adjacent to $v$ in $G$
($|V| - 1 - \deg_G(v)$ of them) and the edge-vertices incident to $v$
($\deg_G(v)$ of them). These families are disjoint and sum to $|V| - 1$.
\end{proof}

Thus, regardless of $\deg_G(v)$, the degree of $v$ in $C(G)$ is the constant
$|V| - 1$: the original vertices form the unique maximum-degree stratum, and any
two adjacent originals have equal degree --- exactly the setup for
Proposition~\ref{prop:obstruction}.

\section{Two lower bounds for regular graphs}

Let $G$ be $d$-regular. Call $G$ \emph{non-complete} if some pair of distinct
vertices is non-adjacent.

\begin{proposition}[Vertex count]\label{prop:count}
A $d$-regular non-complete graph has $|V(G)| \ge d + 2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix non-adjacent distinct $a, b$. The set $\{a, b\} \cup N_G(a)$ has size $d + 2$:
$a$ is not in its own neighbourhood and $b \notin N_G(a)$. Hence $|V| \ge d + 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No AVD colouring with $d+2$ colours]\label{thm:noavd}
Let $G$ be $d$-regular and non-complete. Then $C(G)$ has no AVD-total colouring
with $d + 2$ colours.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix non-adjacent distinct $a, b$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:inj}, an AVD-total
colouring with $d + 2$ colours restricts injectively on the star at $a$, of size
$\deg_{C(G)}(a) + 1 = |V|$; so $|V| \le d + 2$, forcing $|V| = d + 2$ with
Proposition~\ref{prop:count}. Now $a, b$ are adjacent in $C(G)$, both of degree
$|V| - 1 = d + 1$, and the palette size is $(d+1)+1$. Proposition~\ref{prop:obstruction}
rules out the AVD condition.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Degree-governed lower bound]\label{thm:dbound}
For $G$ $d$-regular and non-complete, $\avd(C(G)) \ge d + 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If some AVD-total colouring used $n \le d + 2$ colours, then by
Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} one with exactly $d + 2$ colours would exist, contradicting
Theorem~\ref{thm:noavd}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vertex-governed lower bound]\label{thm:vbound}
For any non-complete graph $G$, $\avd(C(G)) \ge |V(G)| + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix non-adjacent distinct $a, b$; they are adjacent in $C(G)$ of equal degree
$|V| - 1$. If a colouring used $n \le |V|$ colours, Lemma~\ref{lem:mono} yields one
with $|V|$ colours, and Proposition~\ref{prop:obstruction} (with $\Delta = |V|-1$)
forbids the AVD condition on $ab$.
\end{proof}

\section{The extremal regime}

\begin{proposition}[Domination]\label{prop:dom}
For $G$ $d$-regular and non-complete, $d + 3 \le |V(G)| + 1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Proposition~\ref{prop:count}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Agreement iff extremal]\label{prop:agree}
For $G$ $d$-regular and non-complete,
$d + 3 = |V(G)| + 1 \iff |V(G)| = d + 2$.
\end{proposition}

We call a $d$-regular non-complete graph with $|V| = d + 2$ \emph{extremal}.

\begin{proposition}[Complement of a regular graph is regular]\label{prop:compl}
If $G$ is $d$-regular on $n$ vertices, then $G^{c}$ is $(n - 1 - d)$-regular.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
In $G^{c}$ each vertex $v$ is adjacent to the $(n - 1) - \deg_G(v) = n - 1 - d$
vertices other than $v$ to which it was non-adjacent in $G$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Extremal characterisation]\label{thm:char}
Let $G$ be $d$-regular and non-complete. Then $|V(G)| = d + 2$ if and only if
$G^{c}$ is $1$-regular. Equivalently, the extremal graphs are exactly the
cocktail-party graphs $K_{d+2}$ minus a perfect matching.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $|V| = d + 2$ then by Proposition~\ref{prop:compl} the
complement is $(d + 2 - 1 - d) = 1$-regular. ($\Leftarrow$) If $G^{c}$ is
$1$-regular then $|V| - 1 - d = 1$, so $|V| = d + 2$. A $1$-regular graph is a
disjoint union of edges, i.e. a perfect matching; deleting it from $K_{d+2}$ gives
the cocktail-party graph.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp lower bound on the extremal family]\label{thm:sharp}
Let $G$ be $d$-regular and non-complete with $|V(G)| = d + 2$. Then every AVD-total
colouring of $C(G)$ uses at least $d + 3 = |V(G)| + 1$ colours; i.e.
$\avd(C(G)) \ge d + 3 = |V(G)| + 1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:vbound} to get $\ge |V| + 1$, then substitute $|V| = d + 2$.
On the extremal family both bounds coincide, so this single value is the common
floor.
\end{proof}

In the extremal case every original vertex of $C(G)$ has degree exactly $d + 1$.
This is the tight lower half of the guiding conjecture $\avd(C(G)) = d + 3$: the
two competing arguments agree with equality \emph{exactly} on the cocktail-party
family, and the bound is strictly exceeded elsewhere.

\section{Case studies: $C_4$ versus $C_5$}

The $n$-cycle $C_n$ is $2$-regular, so $d = 2$ and extremality reads $n = 4$.

\paragraph{The four-cycle $C_4$ (extremal).} $C_4$ is $2$-regular with
$|V| = 4 = d + 2$, hence extremal. Its complement is $1$-regular (two disjoint
edges, $2K_2$), matching Theorem~\ref{thm:char}. By Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}, every
AVD-total colouring of $C(C_4)$ uses at least $d + 3 = |V| + 1 = 5$ colours. It is
the smallest extremal instance.

\paragraph{The five-cycle $C_5$ (non-extremal).} $C_5$ is $2$-regular with
$|V| = 5 \ne 4$, so it is not extremal. Its complement is $2$-regular
($5 - 1 - 2 = 2$), itself a pentagon. Here the degree bound gives $5$ but the
vertex bound gives $6$, and the latter strictly dominates:
$d + 3 = 5 < 6 = |V(G)| + 1$. The pentagon is the smallest witness that $d + 3$ is
strictly too small off the extremal family.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The governing parameter for $\avd(C(G))$ is the vertex count $|V|$, not the degree
$d$: the vertex-governed bound dominates everywhere, tight only on the
cocktail-party boundary. The interplay of Proposition~\ref{prop:deg} (constant hub
degree) and Proposition~\ref{prop:obstruction} (equal-degree obstruction) converts
a degree hypothesis into a vertex-count conclusion. The non-completeness witness ---
an explicit non-adjacent pair --- is essential: for complete graphs the argument
degenerates.

Open problems, in increasing scope:
(1) a matching upper bound on the cocktail-party family, giving $\avd(C(G)) = d+3$
there;
(2) exact values $\avd(C(C_n))$ for cycles, conjecturally $n + 1$ for $n \ge 4$;
(3) structural corollaries of the extremal characterisation;
(4) a general vertex-governed upper bound $\avd(C(G)) \le |V(G)| + 2$ for all
regular $G$.

